+# Filtering the Veterinarian Directory by Specialty Is In Scope; Free-Text Veterinarian Search Stays Out
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declined searching for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian, on the ground that owner search already demonstrates paged prefix search once and repeating it elsewhere adds surface without understanding. The row was derived from observed absence during the 2026-07-31 bootstrap survey, and its provenance stayed open: whether it was deliberately declined, deferred, or never considered was unknown.
+
+A request has now arrived to narrow the veterinarian portion of the row. It distinguishes two things the original row grouped together: free-text search across an entity, and narrowing a directory by an attribute that directory already displays. The veterinarian directory already shows each veterinarian's specialties; filtering the directory to a chosen specialty reuses data already on the surface rather than adding a new search capability.
+
+The owner has decided (2026-08-26).
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Leave NG-9 whole.** Rejected: it conflates two distinct capabilities. Filtering a displayed attribute is not the free-text entity search the rationale declined, and treating them as one blocks the narrower, cheaper capability along with the broader one.
+2. **Open veterinarian search entirely.** Build free-text search over veterinarians. Rejected: the NG-9 rationale holds for free-text search — it repeats the owner-search pattern without teaching anything new.
+3. **Narrow NG-9 to admit specialty filtering only** (chosen). Filtering the directory by a specialty it already shows is in scope; free-text veterinarian search remains out.
+
+## Decision
+
+NG-9 is narrowed. Free-text search for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian stays out of scope. Filtering the veterinarian directory by a specialty — an attribute the directory already presents — is in scope, and is specified as REQ-VET-003.
+
+The narrowing is bounded to the specialty attribute already on the surface. It does not admit free-text matching, matching on other veterinarian attributes, or search over pets or visits. A later request touching any of those proceeds only on an explicit owner decision recorded at intake.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- NG-9 leaves the open-provenance consultation for the veterinarian dimension: its specialty-filter boundary is now decided. The pet and visit dimensions of NG-9 remain part of the consultation.
+- The veterinarian directory gains a filter capability, carried by REQ-VET-003, across both the page and the machine-readable route.
+- Free-text veterinarian search remains a non-goal. Narrowing it later is a recorded owner decision with its own non-goal ADR, per the Non-Goals table convention.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed NG-9 row.
+- [PRD REQ-VET-003](../prd.md#req-vet-003) — the specialty-filter capability the narrowing admits.
