+### Veterinarian directory routes
+
+The veterinarian directory is served from two routes on `VetController`, both accepting an optional `specialty` query parameter that narrows the listing to veterinarians holding that specialty:
+
+- `/vets.html` renders the paged HTML directory. The `specialty` parameter filters the listing; pagination counts and pages the filtered result, and the page-navigation links carry the parameter so a filtered result stays navigable.
+- `/vets` returns the serialized veterinarian collection (`Vets`). Under REQ-VET-003 this is a supported surface, not the unrequested artifact the withdrawn REQ-VET-002 recorded. It returns all matching veterinarians unpaged — the narrowest reading of the open pagination question in [prd.md](prd.md#open-questions).
+
+Specialty matching is whole-name and case-insensitive. A blank or whitespace-only value behaves as an absent parameter, mirroring empty owner search; an absent or blank parameter leaves both surfaces at their pre-filter behavior. The matching contract is specified by [REQ-VET-003](prd.md#req-vet-003); `VetController` and `VetRepository` are authoritative for the route strings, the parameter name, and the query method.
+
 ### Persistence
 
 The relational schema is not generated from the JPA mapping. `spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto` is set to `none`, and the schema is created by the per-vendor `schema.sql` scripts under `src/main/resources/db/`, with seed rows from the matching `data.sql`. The domain classes carry the JPA mapping annotations directly — there is no separate persistence model and no mapper between them. Under `architecture-principles.md` this is the sanctioned direct-mapping case: the project owns both the model and the schema, and the stored shape tracks the model closely.
